status per paper is kept in authored/evidence-status.json.","citation_ids":["cite.long.record-257bm-k-dich"],"evidence_ids":["mapping.long.record-257bm-k-dich"],"id":"long.record-257bm-k-dich","kind":"claim","label":"Long record Theorem 6.128 (Doubling or returning to an earlier depth)","locator":"Long record Theorem 6.128, p. 79, label record:257bm-k-dich","source_id":"paper.long.pdf","statement":"*For $n\\ge6$, $\\mathrm{ExactLocalMersenneHalfRow}(n)$:\n$$\n\\mathrm{ExactLocalMersenneHalfRow}(2n-1) \\;\\vee\\; \\exists c,\\ 4\\le c\\le n \\wedge \\mathrm{ExactLocalMersenneHalfRow}(2c-2).\n$$\nEvery exact row of depth at least $6$ has at least one of the two asserted continuations. The proof separates the below-half case from the above-half case, but the two existential conclusions need not be exclusive. The returned depth $2c-2$ is not claimed to exceed $n$. The finite Mersenne value cannot equal $1/2$, by Observation 6.138; this says nothing about equality of the returned and original depths. For example, at $n=6$, the support $\\{2,3,6\\}$ is an exact row, and so is $\\{2,3,6,7,11\\}$ at depth $11$. The second conclusion also holds with $c=4$, since $2c-2=6$. Thus both conclusions hold in this example, and the second allows no depth increase. What is missing, per Theorem 6.127, is not another dichotomy but strict endpoint progress in the recycle branch, or a proof the below-half branch recurs ; re-deriving this disjunction without an additional progress argument would not establish cofinality.*","status":"unverified"},"ref":"sha256:264b08973a5f23b2f98ad989b0c1708096513d86929f19048623a2a9fe18092c/nodes/long.record-257bm-k-dich","relations":[{"derived":false,"family":"arguments","from":"sha256:264b08973a5f23b2f98ad989b0c1708096513d86929f19048623a2a9fe18092c/nodes/long.record-257bm-k-dich","id":"sha256:264b08973a5f23b2f98ad989b0c1708096513d86929f19048623a2a9fe18092c/edges/%5B%22long.record-257bm-k-dich%22%2C%22lean.ErdosProblems.Erdos257.PaperCompleteR21.paper_exac